### How do you extract unique words sorted alphabetically?

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class UniqueWordsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String narration = "NEFT transfer to savings NEFT credit from savings";

        // Unique words, sorted alphabetically
        List<String> unique = Arrays.stream(narration.split("\\s+"))
            .map(String::toLowerCase)
            .distinct()
            .sorted()
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Unique (sorted): " + unique);
    }
}
```

```text
Unique (sorted): [credit, from, neft, savings, to, transfer]
```

### How do you preserve first-occurrence order?

```java
// Unique words in first-seen order
List<String> ordered = Arrays.stream(narration.split("\\s+"))
    .map(String::toLowerCase)
    .collect(Collectors.toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new))
    .stream()
    .toList();

System.out.println("Unique (first-seen): " + ordered);
```

```text
Unique (first-seen): [neft, transfer, to, savings, credit, from]
```

### How is distinct() different from LinkedHashSet?

| Approach | Output Order | Memory |
|----------|-------------|--------|
| `distinct().sorted()` | Alphabetical | O(n) for distinct + O(n) for sort |
| `distinct()` alone | Encounter order (source order) | O(n) |
| `toCollection(LinkedHashSet::new)` | First-occurrence order | O(k) where k = distinct count |

`distinct()` alone already preserves encounter order in ordered streams. Adding `sorted()` reorders alphabetically. The LinkedHashSet approach is equivalent to `distinct()` for ordered streams but collects into a Set for downstream use.

### What about punctuation?

```java
// Handle punctuation: split by non-word characters
String withPunctuation = "Account-holder: Rajesh, Priya. Account-holder: Amit.";

List<String> words = Arrays.stream(withPunctuation.split("[^a-zA-Z]+"))
    .map(String::toLowerCase)
    .filter(s -> !s.isEmpty())
    .distinct()
    .toList();
// [account, holder, rajesh, priya, amit]
```

### What are common mistakes?

WRONG: Splitting by a single space instead of `\\s+`.

```java
// WRONG: split(" ") fails on multiple spaces or tabs
String messy = "NEFT  transfer\tto  savings";
String[] wrong = messy.split(" ");  // produces empty strings!
```

CORRECT: Use `\\s+` to handle any whitespace.

```java
// CORRECT: \\s+ handles multiple spaces, tabs, newlines
String[] correct = messy.split("\\s+");
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `distinct()` uses an internal `HashSet` that grows with the number of distinct elements. For very large text processing, consider streaming the text lazily with `BufferedReader.lines()` instead of loading it all into memory.
- For locale-aware case normalization, use `toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)` to avoid the Turkish-i problem where `"I".toLowerCase()` produces a dotless i in Turkish locale.
- The `split` method compiles the regex each time. For repeated calls, pre-compile with `Pattern.compile("\\s+").splitAsStream(sentence)` for better performance.
